WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
The first film showed decomposers draining the leaf pile.

2
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.340
But how fast does a real forest's drain run?

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.020
How could you measure matter you cannot see leaving?

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.850
Researchers in West Virginia found a way.

5
00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:24.110
In October 2011, they worked in the Weimer Run watershed, near the town of Davis.

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:29.663
They sealed dried yellow birch leaves in mesh bags, and weighed the leaves first.

7
00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:34.903
Then they set the bags on the forest floor, at three heights above the stream.

8
00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:39.297
Five times over two years, they brought some bags back.

9
00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:42.317
Different bags came back each time.

10
00:00:43.497 --> 00:00:45.497
They dried the leaves and weighed them again.

11
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.420
Why dry the leaves before weighing?

12
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:50.960
Wet leaves are heavier.

13
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.920
Drying them means that water in the leaves does not change the result.

14
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:58.653
Let's follow the higher site.

15
00:00:59.953 --> 00:01:02.593
The graph shows the percent of the starting mass left.

16
00:01:03.913 --> 00:01:06.033
Every bag started at 100 percent.

17
00:01:07.353 --> 00:01:10.333
Each later dot is an average of 8 or 9 bags.

18
00:01:11.653 --> 00:01:14.553
After one month, about 72 percent was left.

19
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:17.297
That first drop was fast.

20
00:01:18.637 --> 00:01:23.297
Part of it was probably rain washing sugars and other substances out of the fresh

21
00:01:23.297 --> 00:01:26.097
leaves. This is called leaching.

22
00:01:27.357 --> 00:01:29.037
It does not need decomposers.

23
00:01:30.117 --> 00:01:31.817
Now jump to 12 months.

24
00:01:33.097 --> 00:01:35.237
About 60 percent of the mass was left.

25
00:01:36.377 --> 00:01:38.677
So about 40 percent was gone in one year.

26
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.720
At 24 months, a little under half was left.

27
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.780
The bags were still losing mass, but more slowly than in the first year.

28
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.440
One more thing. The mesh kept earthworms and millipedes out.

29
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.400
On an open forest floor, would leaves break down faster or slower than in the bags?

30
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:12.000
Probably faster. Worms and millipedes tear dead leaves into smaller bits.

31
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.980
Smaller bits give fungi and bacteria more surface to work on.

32
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.700
So the bags probably show the drain running slower than it really does.

33
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:27.573
In these bags, about 40 percent drained away in a year, and about half in two.

34
00:02:28.853 --> 00:02:32.953
Suppose a model drain took 40 percent of the whole pile every year.

35
00:02:34.093 --> 00:02:37.093
The pile would level off near 2.5 autumns' worth.

36
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:40.913
But these bags lost mass more slowly in year two.

37
00:02:42.173 --> 00:02:45.033
So two years cannot tell us how big a real pile gets.

38
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.560
Some early loss was probably leaching, and decomposers did some too.

39
00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.720
Weighing measures all the loss together.

40
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.080
It cannot split decomposers from leaching or bits lost through the mesh.

41
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.920
And it shows one kind of leaf in bags, not a whole forest floor.

42
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.140
Weighing leaf bags measures how fast leaves lose mass.
